WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:00.920
Hey my friends.

00:00.920 --> 00:06.480
So we have learned that in real data projects, if you have a database, there will be a lot of analytical

00:06.480 --> 00:10.280
use cases that want to access your data and do analytics.

00:10.280 --> 00:11.160
And what can happen.

00:11.160 --> 00:16.840
They can write complex queries because in many scenarios they are doing complex analyses.

00:16.880 --> 00:21.720
And if you don't do anything about it in your projects, you're going to face a lot of challenges like

00:21.720 --> 00:28.720
complexity and a lot of redundancy of the same complex logic, but from multiple users and maybe performance

00:28.720 --> 00:29.920
and security issues.

00:29.920 --> 00:32.680
And we have learned we have five amazing techniques.

00:32.800 --> 00:34.840
In order to solve those problems.

00:34.840 --> 00:42.320
We have learned the subqueries and cities and as well how to create objects like views, CTAs and temporary

00:42.320 --> 00:42.960
tables.

00:42.960 --> 00:47.000
So now what we're going to do, we're going to go and compare them side by side in order to have a big

00:47.000 --> 00:51.320
picture about the advantages and the disadvantages of each method.

00:51.320 --> 00:52.920
So let's go and compare them okay.

00:52.960 --> 00:54.720
So now we have our five methods.

00:54.720 --> 00:58.960
And the first criteria that I would like to compare them is the storage type.

00:58.960 --> 01:03.280
We have learned that if you are using subqueries and CTE what can happen.

01:03.280 --> 01:09.590
The database can put the result of those two techniques in the memory in the cache, so that later the

01:09.590 --> 01:13.110
main query has a fast access to those intermediate results.

01:13.110 --> 01:19.230
But in the other hand, if we are using temporary tables or tables from the new created table can be

01:19.230 --> 01:21.470
stored inside the disk storage.

01:21.510 --> 01:22.750
And now for the views.

01:22.790 --> 01:28.190
As we understood, there will be no data stored and that means we are not using any storage from the

01:28.190 --> 01:28.950
database.

01:28.990 --> 01:31.110
Now if you are talking about the lifetime.

01:31.110 --> 01:35.270
So that means how long the object can live or persist in the database.

01:35.270 --> 01:39.230
Now our three techniques subqueries, CTE and temporary tables.

01:39.390 --> 01:42.230
All of them can live a short time in the database.

01:42.230 --> 01:44.070
So all of them are temporary.

01:44.070 --> 01:50.070
But now if you are talking about creating objects using CTAs and views, those two can be permanent.

01:50.070 --> 01:54.550
So that means they're going to live in the database as long as you don't drop them.

01:54.550 --> 01:59.430
Now we're going to compare them with something similar is when the database is going to go and drop

01:59.430 --> 02:01.190
or delete those objects.

02:01.230 --> 02:05.550
Now we have learned that the subqueries and the cities have a short time.

02:05.550 --> 02:09.060
They can live only during the execution of the query.

02:09.060 --> 02:13.700
So once the query ends, the database is going to go to the cache and delete everything.

02:13.700 --> 02:19.100
But for the temporary tables, they leave a little bit longer as long as you are in the session.

02:19.100 --> 02:24.300
But once you end the session, the database is going to go and drop and delete your table.

02:24.300 --> 02:27.540
Now for the objects that comes from the CTAs and views.

02:27.580 --> 02:32.940
As we learned, they are persistent and permanent and the database can only delete them if you ask the

02:32.940 --> 02:36.620
database to do that by using the DDL command drop.

02:36.620 --> 02:39.180
So the database will not delete anything for these two.

02:39.220 --> 02:41.700
So now the next one is the query scope.

02:41.740 --> 02:44.100
Like how we can access those objects.

02:44.140 --> 02:46.140
Now for the subquery and the CTE.

02:46.180 --> 02:47.780
The scope is here very small.

02:47.980 --> 02:54.100
It is accessed only from one single query, the query itself where you write the city and subquery.

02:54.100 --> 02:56.900
So you cannot access it from external queries.

02:56.900 --> 03:03.500
But we have learned that the temporary table CTAs and views you can access all those objects from multiple

03:03.500 --> 03:04.260
queries.

03:04.260 --> 03:08.860
So that means you can access those objects from multiple external queries.

03:08.900 --> 03:14.480
Now the next one If you are thinking about the reusability, if you look to the subqueries, they are

03:14.480 --> 03:15.400
very limited.

03:15.440 --> 03:20.280
The subquery is going to be used only in one query and only in one place.

03:20.280 --> 03:24.400
So if you need it in multiple places, you have to go and repeat the same logic.

03:24.400 --> 03:27.280
So subqueries are the worst with their usability.

03:27.280 --> 03:30.200
But now if you are talking about the CTE, it is a little bit better.

03:30.200 --> 03:36.120
You still can access it only from one single query, but you can access it in the same query from multiple

03:36.120 --> 03:40.080
places, so you can access it multiple times from different joins.

03:40.080 --> 03:43.200
And you don't have to repeat the same logic over and over.

03:43.200 --> 03:47.880
But still, it is limited because you have only one query that is using the logic.

03:47.880 --> 03:53.200
Now, if you think about the temporary tables, I could say the reusability here is medium, and that's

03:53.200 --> 03:59.000
because you can access the data by multiple queries, but only during this session.

03:59.000 --> 04:04.560
So once the session is ended, you cannot access it anymore, which means you have to recreate it in

04:04.560 --> 04:06.080
order to reuse it again.

04:06.080 --> 04:12.950
So it is more reusable than the CTE and the subqueries, but not that good like the CTAs and views Those

04:12.950 --> 04:19.390
techniques can offer the highest reusability for you, so they are always there for multiple users from

04:19.390 --> 04:20.670
multiple queries.

04:20.670 --> 04:23.310
So it can eliminate a lot of redundancies.

04:23.310 --> 04:25.190
And you have to do the job only once.

04:25.230 --> 04:29.630
Now moving into the next one, if you are thinking about the intermediate result of those techniques,

04:29.630 --> 04:32.390
the question is how fresh is the data?

04:32.390 --> 04:35.110
Is the data from these objects always up to date?

04:35.150 --> 04:41.110
Now for the subqueries and the cities, they are always up to date because the SQL is executing the

04:41.110 --> 04:44.310
logic on the fly and storing the data in the memory.

04:44.310 --> 04:47.550
And immediately after that going to come the main query and get the data.

04:47.550 --> 04:51.310
So always the intermediate results in the memory are up to date.

04:51.310 --> 04:56.990
But now if you think about that temporary tables and the CTAs, the query is only executed once.

04:56.990 --> 05:03.390
And if there is like an update and changes on the original table, you will not find those changes in

05:03.390 --> 05:04.190
those objects.

05:04.190 --> 05:07.030
And that's because SQL executed once and that's all.

05:07.030 --> 05:11.550
So if you query those tables, there is no guarantee that the data are up to date.

05:11.550 --> 05:17.140
So if you want a fresh data, you have always to drop the table and create it again from the query.

05:17.180 --> 05:19.540
Now if you are talking about the views, they are amazing.

05:19.540 --> 05:23.220
They are always up to date because views does not store any data.

05:23.220 --> 05:28.580
So each time you ask the views for data what can happen, the database is going to go to the original

05:28.620 --> 05:30.740
table and fetch the data to the view.

05:30.740 --> 05:33.380
So your data are always fresh and up to date.

05:33.380 --> 05:39.260
So this is a big picture about the behavior of those advanced techniques that you can use in SQL projects.

05:39.260 --> 05:43.540
And if you ask my opinion, my favorite is going to be the views in the first place.

05:43.540 --> 05:46.020
Then in the second in my list is the CTE.

05:46.300 --> 05:49.980
They are amazing, but don't use more than five cities in one query.

05:50.140 --> 05:52.500
Otherwise it's going to be really annoying and hard to read.

05:52.540 --> 05:56.820
And then I'm going to say in the third place, the subqueries and then the cities.

05:56.860 --> 05:59.260
I use cities if the views are slow.

05:59.380 --> 06:04.780
If that's a scenario, I'm jump to the cities and create a permanent physical tables for my query.

06:04.780 --> 06:08.540
And the last one that I rarely use is the temporary tables.

06:08.540 --> 06:12.180
So this is how I rank those techniques in my SQL projects.

06:16.220 --> 06:20.490
Now I would like to show you as well a big picture on how things works in my projects.

06:20.490 --> 06:24.690
In order to see all those different techniques and possibilities that you can use.

06:24.690 --> 06:26.570
It's like a big picture and recap.

06:26.650 --> 06:27.850
So story time.

06:27.850 --> 06:33.730
So you have a database and things starts where you have a database administrator or let's say data engineer.

06:33.730 --> 06:39.450
That is creating a new table from the scratch so he can write a DDL statement in order to create one

06:39.450 --> 06:41.530
physical table at our database.

06:41.570 --> 06:43.450
And now our database table is empty.

06:43.490 --> 06:48.930
That's why in the second step, you're going to go and write an Insert statement in order to fill our

06:48.930 --> 06:50.330
new table with data.

06:50.370 --> 06:55.330
Now once we have a table, we're going to give the access maybe to a data scientist or data analyst

06:55.330 --> 06:57.690
in order to start writing SQL queries.

06:57.690 --> 07:01.010
So now the first thing that could happen is that the logic is complex.

07:01.010 --> 07:03.210
And she has to do that in two steps.

07:03.370 --> 07:08.850
So the first step is a query that prepares the data in order to execute the second step.

07:08.850 --> 07:11.450
So that's why she's going to go and use the subquery.

07:11.450 --> 07:16.210
And the main query is going to go and retrieve the data from the intermediate results in order to prepare

07:16.210 --> 07:18.530
the final results for the analyst.

07:18.570 --> 07:23.790
Now what could happen is that there will be an SQL logic in the query where it keep repeating in the

07:23.790 --> 07:24.270
scripts.

07:24.310 --> 07:29.590
So now instead of writing another sub query for that, she's going to go and put this logic in CTE.

07:29.790 --> 07:35.430
And now she's going to go to the main query and use the result of the CTE in multiple places in the

07:35.430 --> 07:36.270
same query.

07:36.270 --> 07:41.550
So all those stuff, the sub queries and the CTE queries, the main queries, all those stuff happens

07:41.550 --> 07:43.070
in one single query.

07:43.110 --> 07:47.070
And now what could happen is that she is writing an amazing code.

07:47.070 --> 07:50.550
So instead of using it only in her query, what can happen?

07:50.550 --> 07:54.070
Is she going to go and persist this logic in the database?

07:54.230 --> 08:00.190
So she can put it as a view in the database, so that all other users and analysts can benefit from

08:00.190 --> 08:02.470
this logic, and they don't have to write it again.

08:02.470 --> 08:05.190
So instead they're going to go and query the view.

08:05.190 --> 08:07.310
And this makes the life easier.

08:07.310 --> 08:11.590
And of course our data analysts can as well use this view in the main query.

08:11.630 --> 08:12.470
And now one more thing.

08:12.510 --> 08:15.790
She has as well another logic that is really complex.

08:15.950 --> 08:17.990
And as well everyone can benefit from it.

08:17.990 --> 08:20.710
But the issue this query is very slow.

08:20.710 --> 08:27.180
So now she has to decide do I put it in view or do I create a new table based on the query using CTAs?

08:27.340 --> 08:32.100
Now, of course, because of the performance and the view takes around 30 minutes to be executed, she

08:32.100 --> 08:38.020
decided to execute the query using the CTAs, where she generates a physical table so that all other

08:38.020 --> 08:42.780
analysts as well can access this new table in order to reuse the results.

08:42.820 --> 08:45.340
And of course, she can use it in her main query.

08:45.460 --> 08:49.420
And with that, now you have experience how things works in real projects.

08:49.460 --> 08:52.300
It is not simple select query from table.

08:52.300 --> 08:53.300
It is like this.

08:53.300 --> 09:00.060
People are creating subquery cities views, temporary tables, CTAs for different purposes.

09:00.100 --> 09:00.820
All right my friends.

09:00.820 --> 09:01.740
So that's all about that.

09:01.740 --> 09:04.100
CTAs and the temporary tables.

09:04.100 --> 09:08.300
And with that we have learned all the techniques on how to organize our complex projects.

09:08.340 --> 09:11.340
Now next we're going to start talking about something completely different.

09:11.340 --> 09:16.460
We're going to talk about the stored procedures on how to put our code inside a database.

09:16.460 --> 09:22.500
This is all about the programmability and how to add stuff like parameters, variables, error handling.

09:22.500 --> 09:23.860
So it's like programming.

09:23.860 --> 09:24.660
So let's go.

09:24.780 --> 09:28.060
So let's uncover this world of the stored procedures and let's go.
